Runtime capture — feat/mode-staleness-tracking @88628f6 running in the
production bridge (image govee2mqtt:mode-staleness, host with 5 Govee
devices, AWS IoT + LAN + Platform all active).

Setup: cloud power/effect command sent to an H6020 at 18:32:05Z, which
triggers a full AWS IoT status push carrying "mode":5. The device is
LAN-capable, so subsequent LAN polls win the source race and project the
carried-over mode.

Capture 1 — 90 seconds after the IoT push (sensor attributes, HA API):
  iot      mode=5 mode_updated=2026-08-14T18:32:06.406016650Z updated=2026-08-14T18:32:06.406016650Z source=AWS IoT API
  lan      mode=5 mode_updated=2026-08-14T18:32:06.406016650Z updated=2026-08-14T18:33:33.872013013Z source=LAN API
  overall  mode=5 mode_updated=2026-08-14T18:32:06.406016650Z updated=2026-08-14T18:33:33.872013013Z source=LAN API

Capture 2 — ~10 minutes after the IoT push, no further IoT traffic:
  iot      mode=5 mode_updated=2026-08-14T18:32:06.406016650Z updated=2026-08-14T18:32:06.406016650Z source=AWS IoT API
  lan      mode=5 mode_updated=2026-08-14T18:32:06.406016650Z updated=2026-08-14T18:41:45.263126318Z source=LAN API
  overall  mode=5 mode_updated=2026-08-14T18:32:06.406016650Z updated=2026-08-14T18:41:45.263126318Z source=LAN API

The LAN projection keeps re-stamping `updated` with each poll while
`mode_updated` stays pinned to the original IoT observation time —
before this change the projection offered no way to tell the two apart.
